[@does the timeline even work out here? well, that seems like a research project to me...!~M]

Bertrand h’Ateaux snapped his rotary phone closed, satisfièdly.[@ I imagine it's a candlestick phone, although I didn't find a place to mention this. ~M] His paymaster was now duly informed of his recent activities, using their conventional code speak known only by the two of them. He would have preferred to use his car telegraph instead of the rotary dial texting, but unfortunately Sonic refused to upgrade her carrier to the new network that would have allowed her to receive both telephone and telegram — Multimedia Messaging Service (MMS).

Bertrand leaned back, relaxed, in his Aeros̈peedẅagon[@ (get it?)[@ Is that a Jo-Jo reference?[@ Kidding. Though there is apparently a Jo-Jo character called Robert E. O. Speedwagon. ~C] ~C] ~M], the flying car he had used to get here[$he had usen to got here?]. His obligation, his compliance and reporting portion, his KYC and AML and KFC and XML requirements, dispatched with, he could jam out in the cab without a care in the world. Sonic was such a Luddite,[$ and, indeed, as a bonus character unlockable in FIPC's Mario Kart, technically also a ludite[$ludoïte?]] he thought to himself. Indeed, she even still hoarded Bitcoin. And she kept it in— oh, hello!

Bertrand's train of thought was interrupted as his car darkroom dinged[$dung?] and spat out a photograph, from a slot near the glove compartment. Bertrand took the photo in his hot little hands[$ "He come to me with money in his hand!" —Charles Manson —Death Grips] and turned it over. As he was examining it, he shook it — a common Skoios superstition that makes focusing on a photo temporarily very difficult. After he was done making a mockery of the visual arts, he steadied the photo and examined it. It was a picture of a beautiful woman, a glamorous black-and-white headshot. Written on it in white was the text "Take my wife, please! —Whitehead".[@ This character's name is a reference to _Principia Mathematica_. ~M]

Bertrand chuckled. That Whitehead, what a card! He was communicating, of course, to Bertrand in a sort of code. Not the aforementioned secret code, but just that way that friendships sort of accrue in-jokes like teeth accrue plaque or like Bertrand's wall of honors accrues plaques. Rendering incapable of moving without the means of such things — although now that he considered the metaphor[$ that's right, he's the one thinking of this metaphor in the story!] neither of those things moved in the first place. And he hoped they never would.

But the point was, he knew exactly what this message meant. He tore up the photograph — more where those came from — in a display of carefree jouissance that only the truly affluent can muster. While he was an assistant to Sonic — a type of servant — he also had servants of his own, like Machine. This was how the Flying Island society had solved the "servant problem": recursive servitude.[@ I'm not sure that is recursive, strictly speaking. Hierarchical. Feudal, perhaps.[$ especially if they're hillbillies.[@ Does putting a footnote in an annotation even work?[@ Yeah. ~M] Certainly not that useful for the longterm. Maybe I'll change 'em all to footnotes at somepoint. ~C]] ~C] That, and the drink.

[@ In my mind the woman is Stacy B. Horatio (the B stands for Bird), but if that doesn't happen then we'll circle back and put that into NCN %% ~M]

[C; 1321 words]

[@ bury me in the banana backwoods, boys ~C ❡ in this handoff I did a lot of inconsequential describing, introduced a new character we'll have to deal with, and advanced the plot like half a notch ~C ❡ perhaps my best work ~C ❡ I'm always like, 'this time I will write a mere 300 words, I don't really know what to write but the good news is I don't have to write much' and then I start ideating ~C ❡ "Too many ideas." —Critics ~M ❡ they hate I(deating) cuz they ain't I(deating) ~C ❡  I(dea)t(ing) ~M ❡ Ideaäting ~M ❡ In my mind, although I suppose this wasn't reflected in the concrete text, Bertrand was actually already at the destination and was sitting in his car alone waiting for Reginald to show up. So you've actually advanced the plot half a notch backwards! ~M ❡ niiiiice ~C]

His joissance evolved to full-fledged fervorvescence. That's right, he'd been going to jam! And when the Randman jammed, everyone was jelly.[$ Bertrand is a little silly.]

Of course, the Randman could only play air guitar, but he considered himself somewhat expert.

Music filled the luxurious cabin of the Aeros̈peedẅagon. The written word can scarcely do justice[^justice] to the quality of this music, like eels[$ jellied, possibly] twisting, twinning in midair, splitting, reforming, seeming to paint silver swirls on the back of your eyes, then to take a brush of comet-tails to the dark canvas of the night, then making mockery of the distinction.

Since the written word can't do that—I think we can all agree that description was a little forced, more flowery than evocative[$ the trick to good writing is to lampshade your foibles, turning them into self-aware humorous humilty.[$ And you just keep adding more lampshades.]]—let's talk about the gymnastic feats[$ using his gymnastic feets] h’Ateaux was performing as he played. The twisting of his spine would have pressed an envious groan from an accordian. He bounced like a rubber ball, with incredible vigor for a man of his rotundity.[$Rotondoto, even.] Beforehand, one couldn't have been certain he even *had* a spine. His manic energy was quite out of keeping with the ethereal grace of the music. Oh, nevermind. "Ethereal grace." I guess words could do it after all, nice.

With a last flourish (a backwards bounce off the back wall of the compartment, coming to rest on his knees on the floor while his fingers danced spastically and then: slower, slower, slower, as the last few chords flowed out), Bertrand concluded his performance. Puffing, he got to his feet (a man of more statuesque build would have brushed his pate on the ẅagon's ceiling) and took a bow. He with blushes waved away a wholly theoretical shout of "encore!" and resumed his seat.

The curtain of smoked, noise-dampening water split down the middle, allowing communication between the luxurious driving compartment and the still more opulent passenger lounge.[@ Seems to me that readers will have thought Bertrand was in the passenger or driver seat from the last section where it talks about the glove compartment, so maybe we should go back and mention the layout earlier or something ~J[@ Probably, but I don't want to deal with that at the moment. ~C]] Bertrand smiled and winced at the discordant skrangs[@ wat ~J[@ am I not allowed to coin novel onomatopoeia? ~C]] now unveiled. Hans Rectifier[$ No relation to the rectifying knob seen in the previous chapter[$ *Or is there?!*]] Machine Gans-Loos[^@name] was applauding.[^sound system]

The manservant twisted in his seat beside the chaüffer and reached his chunky forelimbs through the gap. "Marvelous, sir," he intoned. "Shall I tend to the instrument?"

"Yes, Machine, see to it." Bertrand unslung the transparent strap and handed the invisible instrument over to the waiting clamps, which grasped it with delicacy.

Hans withdrew into the driver's compartment and flicked the water curtain back on. With the pneumatic hisses and whirs that accompanied his every movement, he began the complex techniques required to maintain the air guitar.[$Counterintuneitively, tuning an air guitar is most effective directly after playing the instrument. Something something glassine settling something something resonance modulus something.]

A Skoion[@ have we already established an adjectival form of Skoios? If so, I have forgotten it. ~C] air guitar would look, if it looked at all, like the bastard of a 21st century electric guitar and a chambered nautilus, or a mandolin by H. R. Geiger, or perhaps a guitar Ship-of-Theseused with the parts of a pipe organ. It is the grandchild of the glass harp, that delicate instrument in which music is coaxed out by stroking one's fingers on the rims of an arrangement of wine glasses, but air guitar:glass harp::electric guitar:piano forte. The fundamental accoustic mechanism is the same, but designed for the stage rather than the parlor. The substance conjured by the materials science of the d's[$ the hexadecade beginning 0x2d [$ 0d45] years before our story did] to possess the balance of strength, weight, and resonance optimal for the hitherto-impractical air guitar had—by pure happenstance—certain interesting optical properties as well: to wit, transparency to visible light and the same refractive index as air.

The end result is an instrument almost wholly invisible to sight and which sounds, some have said, like a theremin which has lost its virginity.

As he worked, Hans marveled at the performance he had just been privileged to hear. To be able to extemporize such profound beauty... He shook his head and nudged a protrusion, causing slithery things and minute sounds to happen all over the guitar. The many vacuoles, lacuna, vessels and valves of an air guitar[^orgy tar] admit of a bewildering hyperdimensionality in the customization of the timbre and render caring for the instrument nearly as difficult as playing one. Bertrand and Hans were each renowned masters of their respective crafts.

After a few minutes of silence, textured on one side by panting, on the other by further minute sounds from the guitar, the water curtain again parted, allowing the silences to mix and mingle for a moment.

"We're coming up on it, sir," said the chaüffer, turning his head a fraction.

Harris Teetering was a recent addition to the h'Ateaux staff. A quiet and unassuming young man with mousy brown hair. After a sentence like that, further description is hardly necessary. He had not exactly distinguished himself during the interview with Hans—he never distinguished himself—but during the practical he drove[$ druv? or should it be piloted?] in the exact manner desired by Bertrand: calmly, safely, and very very fast. He sailed without blip through the rest of screening process, which did not include an x-ray portion, and was hired.

In the interest of not shocking delicate readers, and ensuring a proper foundation of what I believe is called 'foreshadowing' before all 'twists', it may be best to say openly now that Harris Teetering is a sleeper agent, activation phrase 'white-taupe rocker'. But perhaps I've said too much. It probably won't come up unless things get really out of hand or there's a very unlikely coincidence. Best to put it out of your mind.

The assurance that the high-yield thermobaric[$ Yes, thermobaric. It's complicated and I'm not going to explain how it works here because, as has been stated, it probably won't come up.] device nestled cozily in his abdomen by his liver will not detonate during the remainder of this chapter bears my personal guarantee.

"Jolly good!" cried Bertrand, moving with a bound and a scoot to the window. He spotted his destination at once. There. Scudding with easy, domineering languor, Skoios balanced on the horizon. A literal city skyline. It was already perceptibly larger than it had been when Bertrand first moved to the window: Harris piloted [$ pilöt?] very fast indeed.

On the other side of the sky[$ not literally; the aerobike was actually closer to the Aeros̈peedẅagon than the vast majority of other objects aloft in the world], the tiny speck that was Reginald sped toward the same destination.[@ Wait. This doesn't make any sense, does it. Unless the Digsxby island has drifted very far from Skoios. Or at least as far as wherever Bertrand is coming from. Which would be pretty weird; I feel like all the stuff we've written implies it's still pretty close. Maybe Harris goes super ultra fast. I'ma leave it for now, but this may warrant revisiting. ~C][@ To be honest I’m not really sure what any of this implies about distances ~J]

[^justice]: Whoa, easy there, Sonic! It's just[$ Dang it, Sonic, we've talked about this in therapy. People can use the word "just", it's a versatile word in English.[$ Sonic meta-textual powers: not confirmed as canon. She's not Thomas, after all. Sonic therapy[$ Sonic therapy. Sounds like it should be a thing. Maybe some kinda music-based—Oh, right, we should back to the story. Quite forgot myself.] for not firing up at people using the root 'just-':[@ This should probably be `‘just-’` ~M ❡  Oh, the not-backward apostrophe? ~C [@ 🙂‍↕️ ~M] ❡ It also shows up around Orgy Tar ~M ❡ same ~C [@ 🙂‍↕️ ~M] ❡ Also white-taupe rocker ~M ❡  it shows up every time I have single quotes. ~C ❡ but I'll never stop ~C ❡ [@ ❤️ ~M]] eh, I kinda like it, we'll see what the commitee says.]] an expression.

[^@name]: I put possibly too much effort into this name being a nice parallel of Manuel Ordinator Computer Gonzalez. 1) Hans sounding like 'hands' vs. Manuel sounding like 'manual'. 2) I don't know the rationale, if any, behind 'Ordinator', but in addition to meaning something which orders/arranges elements, it also means someone who can ordain a person to the clergy. Rectifier is a kind of electrical circuit (if I remember rightly, it converts AC to DC) but it also sounds like someone who could make someone into a Rector, an administrative leader in some Christian denominations. 3) Machine was provided by J in Digsxby Excapes; it seemed like someone in the same position as Manuel, so that's what I'm running with. 4) Gans and Loos are Dutch surnames, and together sound a bit like Gonzalez. Hans is a first name in a lot of nationalities besides Dutch, so a search of Germanic or Danish surnames, for example, might turn up a better match, but I only had the patience for Dutch.[@ Beautiful ~J] ~C

[^sound system]: 'How could Hans hear the music if there was noise-cancelling water(?) in the way?' I hear you ask. To which I answer: Shut up. Who asked you.[$ If you must know, there's a dial under Bertrand's stubby fingers which controls the directionality of the noise-cancellation, whether one way[$for private conferences], t'other[$ for issuing instructions without talk-back or irritating breathing], both, or neither. And a switch to lock/unlock the driver-side ability to open and close the water curtain.]

