“I’ll be asking the questions here!” screamed Georgia Mann dispassionately, “Won’t I?!”
“Yes, madam.” came the reply, slightly robotic out of habit.
Manuel felt naked without his robot costume. Indeed, he was naked without it. Georgia had insisted the silly thing be removed, not realizing he wasn’t wearing anything else, and now she felt it would lose her ground in the interrogation to order clothes brought for him. It was technically a legal interrogation tactic to keep him in the nude as long as the room was a comfortable temperature and the chair had been sanitized and various other stipulations had been observed, none of the details of which was anyone present was aware of.[@ I think one of the latter two 'was'es in this sentence should be deleted. ~C] Georgia simply remembered it was technically legal for her to make him be naked and tried to use the awkward mishappenstance to her advantage. It was also legal on Skoios to scream at, cry in front of, poke, slap, and bite an arrestee, within reason. The designated interrogation referee would intervene if anything seemed to cross the line.
“I want to know why you killed Georg Smelv.” Mann said cooly.
“I did not kill Georg Smelv.” Manuel replied, finding it easiest to remain civil rather than expose the vulnerability of his outrage.
“I want to know why you pushed Archbishop John Winterpipe up that bridge.”
“*Off* the bridge, I think you—”
“Aha!” Georgia screeched triumphantly, trying to determine if this would be an effective moment to bite him, and deciding against it, “How did you know he was pushed if you didn’t push him?”
“Why would I push someone up a bridge?”
“That’s what I’d like to know, you sick freak,” Georgia snarled, eyeing his pale shoulder. She imagined sinking her teeth into it just hard enough to make him wonder if she would draw blood. She was a good biter, and she knew what to do, although her victims/suspects were usually clothed.
Manuel didn’t respond to her accusation. He just looked at her in disbelief, an expression which intensified significantly when she struck and bit him on the shoulder. “Ow!” he cried, and wondered if she would draw blood.

The interrogation referee looked on impassively.

---

Francesca looked on impassively as two strange men tried to tune her piano.

