[@ this (sub?)section might be called "Piano". "Piano planes"."Prudence senses an infinite tableau vivant of significance stretching thru time and space as she observes the two men incompetently tuning the piano and suddenly senses their significance." ]

[@ this part is vaguely inspired by the first chapter of Seiobo There Below, which I read around this time. ❡ However, this is not a full pastiche of it, or I would have used more semicolons.]

    Prudence looked on passively[@ this is a joke: first of all it's (verbally) the opposite of impassively. Even though both of them are doing nothing and thus both are passive. Secondly, she is in fact very affected (as opposed to an impassive person, who is unaffected).] as two strange men tried to tune Francesca's piano.
    The men worked in silence, _[:lang:it piano] [:lang:pseudo-it pianississimo]_, and neither seemed to be intending to collaborate with the other. Their hands roved over[@arguably this preposition is quite redundant] the entire surface of the piano. Their stances varied and shifted from time to time, from one unusual, improbable pose to the next, minute after minute. In complete silence. It came to resemble a sort of devilish dance, or infernal pantomime, in the slowest of motions. The very macarena they do in Hell. 
    As Prudence watched this slow and stationary procession, it seemed (mentally, purely in a sort of mental sort of way) to expand: first back and forth of itself, creating an infinite series, an infinite number of pianos and pianomen, stretching from each end of the endless universe to the other, not even compromising its curveless curve for the bend of the surface of the earth; then that line itself repeated an infinite number of times back and forth, surging backwards indefinitely and forwards consuming even the space where Prudence et alia[@ this is "et al." but it specifically is the feminine singular because it refers to Franny; unfortunately (?) the common form using the plural neuter, (et alia) is spelled exactly the same way, so it's impossible for the reader to tell I didn't mean that. Unless I were to use ruby interlinear annotation text.... but I am le tired. ❡ Although, I suppose I should use altera, to homage the dual case...? %% ❡ this is also the root word of "adultery", which is suggestive... probably won't be able to use that for anything tho.] stood[$standen?] — not that her mind made any particular allowance for this — to tile the plane, as far as the eye could see. The surroundings seemed to become dark, as though the lights were out and a spotlight rested only upon the piano and the men. And then this beẗiled plane rose and descended infinitely, plunged up and down in a languid instant, like as it were floors of a very large building, the _[:lang:it pianos]_ of an infinitely large building,[$ in Italy they call storeys [:ilang:it pianos], fun fact] until the _[:lang:fr tableau vivant]_ beẅitnessed fill'd the space entire, copied to innumerable identical, numerically identical,[@ "numerically identical" is a (confusing) philosophy term of art for "actually really the same thing (not just the same in every external property)"] _[:lang:fr tableaux vivants]_, filling the space holey[@intentional pun here. There is space around the piano] and completely, the space sweltering with it, absolutely dripping with the humidity of these retards.
    And here her spacial imagination stopped for she was but a poor three-dimensional creature, and not too taken to fractal spaces neither, but she began to sense, in this hallucinatory vision, that the cataḧoneyc̈ombs that ensnared the world, of these two oafs oafing, extended as it were in time as well, and happened always, in every instant of time from the beginning of endless time to its endless end, like God, like the Backrooms, like The Infinite City, like the risible doctrines of the B-time-theorists on the subject of moments finally made real, like the immortality of David Hume, like the Sheets Of Color,[^@explanations] like the paltry but persistent latent existence of abstract objects, like an insistent annoyance, like treacle, like jam[@ jam tomorrow, jam yesterday, but never any jam today...!], like honey, like papier-mâché, like fog, like compound interest, like you.
    And in this state, in this stage of infinite extension, when she was surrounded as it were on all sides by the subject, the object, and she could examine it at her leisure, on all sides, for as long as she wanted, and size it up up and down, and so on, although it could not have been more than a couple minutes really, in this state most becoming[@tempted to write bec̈oming here, but I think that would be wrong. That word is lexicalized into its current form; I'm not trying to pull a Heideggerian move like ex-istence here.] investigation, that is, Prudence... Realized.
    [@ personally I assume she's realized that these guys are not piano tuners. Although it is for the author who writes it down to determine exactly what it is she Realized. It could even be something about the Backrooms, I suppose. Idk what it would be, though. Anyway, this part is written on an ending beat where I expect the next author to cut away. But it's up to the next author to decide that.]
    [^@explanations]: The Infinite City: a Backrooms-like city of infinite bigness and nondescriptness, that has not (yet?) appeared in the novel. The immortality of David Hume: ditto, but for David Hume's real-life wish not to live forever, and my reflections on it. Sheets Of Color: based on a vision my friend had on salvia (which he described as not a good high; just must be something people do because it's the only thing they have), concerning how the world became in his perception not objects but infinite sheets of color he saw from the side.
